{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2503.18983, how does the precautionary principle relate to both due diligence obligations and international cooperation requirements for states?", "answer": "The precautionary principle is linked to states' due diligence obligations to prevent harm and also requires international cooperation to address irreversible threats.", "evidence": [{"page": 28, "quote": "the ITLOS Seabed Dispute Chamber linked the precautionary approach to states' general obligation of due diligence and, on this basis, found that states were 'required . . . to take all appropriate measures to prevent damage that might result from the activities of contractors that they sponsor.'"}, {"page": 29, "quote": "ITLOS, for instance, considered in the MOX Plant Case that 'prudence and caution' require states to cooperate in exchanging information concerning risks and devising ways to deal with those risks."}]},
  {"question": "What does arXiv 2503.18983 state about the reversal of the burden of proof under the precautionary principle, and how is this applied to AI developers?", "answer": "The precautionary principle can shift the burden of proof to those wishing to carry out potentially hazardous activities, including AI developers, requiring them to prove safety before market placement.", "evidence": [{"page": 30, "quote": "the precautionary principle could also in theory operate as a reversal of the burden of proof, from the entity opposing the conduct of a potentially hazardous activity to the one that wishes to carry it out."}, {"page": 30, "quote": "it is logical to transfer the burden of proof to AI developers, as they should fully understand the threat posed by their products before placing them on the market."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2503.18983, what is the 'Anti-Catastrophe Principle' and how does Sunstein justify its application to AI regulation?", "answer": "Sunstein's 'Anti-Catastrophe Principle' supports regulatory intervention for catastrophic risks like AI, even without conclusive evidence, as a large margin of safety is warranted.", "evidence": [{"page": 34, "quote": "Sunstein agrees that 'the Precautionary Principle has a legitimate place when people face a potentially catastrophic risk to which probabilities cannot be assigned.' On this basis, Sunstein advocates applying an 'Anti-Catastrophe Principle' in regulatory policy."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2503.18983, how is the right to life defined under the ICCPR, and what does 'arbitrary' deprivation mean?", "answer": "The right to life protects against 'arbitrary' deprivations, defined as interferences not prescribed by law, disproportionate, or unnecessary given less harmful means.", "evidence": [{"page": 37, "quote": "the Human Rights Committee, the body in charge of monitoring compliance with the ICCPR, has defined an arbitrary deprivation of life as involving an interference that is (1) not prescribed by law, (2) disproportionate to the ends sought, and (3) not necessary in the circumstances of the case considering the availability of less harmful means."}]},
  {"question": "How does arXiv 2503.18983 describe the relationship between AI design and the obligation not to arbitrarily deprive life?", "answer": "AI should be designed to comply with the right to life, prioritizing human rights in its decision-making; if not possible, states must reconsider AI programs' compatibility with this right.", "evidence": [{"page": 38, "quote": "the obligation to not arbitrarily deprive humans of their life compels the design of AI to comply with this right. If this is not possible, then states have to consider more fundamentally the compatibility of ongoing AI programs with the right to life."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2503.18983, what challenge is noted regarding holding private actors responsible for AI existential risks under international human rights law?", "answer": "Currently, only states are legally obligated under international human rights law to address AI existential risks; private actors are not, though this may change in the future.", "evidence": [{"page": 39, "quote": "private actors are therefore under no legal obligation, at least under international human rights law, to take precautions to address AI as an existential threat. At the same time, there is some movement toward a recognition of private actors being subjected to human rights obligations."}]}
]}
